In this paper I will discuss my view on the Technological Utopianism (referred to as TU from now) idea, after which I will describe some improvements to the idea to improve it. The TU stands for a society in which technology is free to rise as much as wanted. At a first glimpse this seems wanted, but there are some (possibly negative) consequences involved with it. The view of the TU concerns me in two areas, namely the full freedom of technological developments and the propagandize of the free market system including as less as possible government control. 

The freedom of technology has until now resulted in several consequences that can be labeled unwanted by many people. First a cruelty in which animals are treated has arisen. Some examples include: machine assisted milking of cows, transportation of big amounts of cattle on trucks and commercial egg production of chickens. Of course one could question whether without technology these examples would've been practiced at current day as well, but it's clear to see from the examples that technology plays are major role in them physically, in other words without the technology being available they'd surely not be practiced the way they are now. Second, a loss of privacy seems to happen because of the implementation of technology at many different areas. Technology has made it easy to trace people in several ways (cell phone connections, webcams on the streets, digital on line traces), it has made it mandatory for people to authorize and thus identify themselves more often (password to log on the internet, credit card purchase online) and has increased the potential threats of private information becoming public property (private information stored in several systems, such as government systems). Third, the ecological degration caused by technology. Every day millions of cars, airplanes, boats and trucks travel over our planet, causing a stream of pollution. All of the previously mentioned vehicles are clearly are result of technological developments and clearly threat the future existence of humanity (global warming). Finally a phenomenon called consumerism showed up. Consumerism is relating the personal happiness to the ability in which they can afford to buy goods. Due to technology it's become possible to increase the productivity of employees and the variety of goods that can be produced. As a result of that different people have different access to scarce sources, causing jealousy and competition in having to own and consume as much as possible. From a wellbeing perspective this is a very negative case and one has to really question of people can ever be satisfied when this phenomenon gets bigger and bigger. Of course technology isn't all about bad things, but what can been seen from this paragraph is that one shouldn't consider it the most normal case in the world to give technology a free path completely.

The second major issue the TU stands for is the previously introduced free market with as less government control as possible. Although a priori this might seem an appealing way to set up a society (let everything be ruled by ask and demand), but for several reason it's not the case. First of all, the principle of ask and demand assumes that people know what they want. This is obviously not the cause because of several reasons. Research has shown that many purchases are done because of  peer pressure (people in the close enviroment expecting you to buy a product. eg. Coca Cola is cool), not exactly knowing what the product they buy offers (this often happends with a push strategy in which the companies decide what consumers buy), advertisements that basically brainwash people and having the ability to show off (my BMW is faster then your Mercedes). As a result of this, companies that have technological know-how can systematically force people to make a certain direction and possibly having a unwantedly big impact on society. For example google.com, a major internet search engine. Imagine that they filter their search results on aspects like religion, gender, social class, race , sexual preference, being disabled etc. This gives them power to shape the world in a way they want. Two events that already happened and showed the massive power of a relatively small group of people are the nuclear bombs that have been dropped on Japan. Second the fact that humans are often focused on short term results instead of thinking about saving humanity. This can be seen in the oil consumption of humans, which is much higher then wanted on long term. Due to high taxes on fuel in Europe, this could still be limited somehow but still not enough, but in case of a completely free market it'd be a lost case. The previously mentioned phenomenon addresses a long term issue, but also on a mid term or short term base, protection of humans by their government is a need. This is expressed in higher prices for alcohol and cigarettes and the total prohibition of drugs in most countries in the world. Other aspects include the availability of social security, affordable education and a nationwide infrastructure, not always appreciated much by the free market economy. Finally strict laws need to be enforced on products that can cause a health hazard. For example clean water is essential and one could argue whether the water industry would be better off in government hands or not. A free market doesn't leave this choice open.

After addressing the dangers of technology and a free market, let's have a look at what TU promises if things go their way. As can be read on the wikipedia article Techno-utopianism, TU results in the end of economic scarcity which induces according them also the end of any major social evil. In the next two paragraphs each of the two promises will be reviewed.

First the removal of economic scarcity. A global trend that can be observed now is that on average every national economy is getting bigger and bigger in terms of gross product which can be considered a good thing, but two other trends called global digital divide and digital divide have also showed up. Both are concerned with the gap between the ones with access to technology and then ones without it getting bigger. This occurs because of the following reasons: people with technology have access to more information and can thus become more educated and well informed in their decisions and make more successful decisions. The global trend shows a geographic separation such as the different continents (Europe, North America and Australia being developed against Asia, Africa and South America developing) while the local division happens betweens the different social classes (e.g. richer people can afford a newspaper). Above this division a previously mentioned aspect named consumerism might cause the economic scarcity to never end. In fact, the more is technologically possible, the bigger people's desires and jealousy might be.

Let's assume that for someone reason the economic scarcity disappears (this also implies that the poorest people have a decent amount of income, which is not likely in a free market economy without social security). TU promises this will also mean that any social evil will disappear. Reasons why this can't be considered the case are there. First there are many examples of rich people that can buy anything they want resorting to stimulants like drugs and alcohol. This can likely be contributed to their lack of scarcity, which makes it unnecessary for them to think well about their financial decisions and thus takes away some challenge in life to perform in for example a job. Of course this issue matters for any social level. Second, even though the poorest people might not have a worry about keeping themselves alive and finding enough entertainment, it might still be very frustrating for them to see that other groups of people have access to more resources then them. The jealousy might cause them to steal from them or harm a richer party to 'give them what they deserve'. Third a misunderstanding between humans might occur, which causes a conflict between them and history has shown that many great wars have started with a small argue. Fourth 
not all human needs can be fulfilled economically, which include among others: sexual needs, needs to boss people around, attention and love. When there's no scarcity, there will be less or no people at all to provide these needs either. Such as a complimental secretary that agrees with everything her boss says. A psychologist that listens carefully as long as you keep paying him/her. Finally no matter the amount of scarcity, there will be always groups of people that stand for a certain believe and won't tolerate people who think differently. These believes can involve religion, sexual preference, race, gender etc.

In the previous part of this paper I've criticised technology and the free market system. After that I've shown what TU stands for and what they promise. I've given reasons why even when the circumstances are what TU wants them to be it's still not possible to fulfil those promises. It's always relatively easy to debate someone's vision, but of course it'd be nice to provide an alternative in return, which I'm going to do next. First I will tell something about technology from my perspective, after which I will describe the market system as I prefer it. Finally I'll explain how to combine those aspects in society.

Technology. As can be read previously it can be very dangerous to give technology a free hand in everything, but it's too pessimistic to say that technology only causes bad things. Technology makes it possible for us to understand the world better and can therefore result in more accurate handling of humans to achieve something. As stated before, technology has caused many negative impacts to this planet, such as animal cruelty and pollution. I believe technology is able to solve these problems as well, but the risk in counting on it too much makes it very likely that humans will just blindly go into the future. A way to solve this is to pretend that technology won't get any better then it is right now. This makes us independent of a need for future progress and forces us to solve our problems right now instead of moving them to the future. Of course it's not possible to solve the problems that technology caused instantly. After all we got used to our living standard which has caused the problem, but it's very important to keep a strict eye on the way in which we want to reduce the harm technology causes right now. Strict deadlines of minimizing pollution, animal abuse etc. need to be made even when it'd mean it'd cost some welfare. This approach will cause that technological developments will only be a bonus in achieving these plans faster. A second point to consider is the really harmful products technology produced like a nuclear bomb. It's impossible to prevent such a thing from being invented, but after they have, access to appliance needs to be restricted as much as possible. In other words, a person can know how to make and use a nuclear bomb, but because of the use of appropriate measurements of limiting access to the physical resources, it's impossible for this person to apply his plan.

Next the market system. As I showed before, the are several reasons why a completely free market with reduced government impact won't do. The government needs to be there for different reasons: First the government needs to make sure everyone can have the minimum standard of living no matter what their circumstances are. This includes the right to have sufficient money, education, social well-being and security. This can't be left to the market, because in every human life random things happen that make it temporarily not possible for that person to function as needed in a market, namely: health problems (both mental and physical), needing to invest in education first before having it pay off, getting fired in a job due reorganisation, losing a crop of food due bad weather conditions etc. Second there are products that need a certain quality or that are not covered well by the free market. Drinking water and electricity are considered primary needs, and releasing those products to the free market includes a big risk. For example drinking water could be of poor quality (health hazard), lack of electricity at some times can cause big damage for business (computer files getting lost). Of course the respective companies can be held responsible for the damage they cause, but this doesn't work for 2 reasons. Health damage might be irreversible and the company might go broke because of the damage claim, which causes other people having to pay for that damage which likely results in a reduced overall welfare. Another product not covered by the free market is the infrastructure of an area. This includes data connections, power connections, roads, railway networks, snail mail delivery, bus routes etc. If this would be up to the market principle, only the populated areas would have these facilities because more people would use them there and thus they'd be more profitable, but when a government has control of this, the right for everyone to have access to them could be preserved.  The previous part of this paragraph gives the impression that government influence is very needed and brings up the question whether it's possible to be extreme and let the government control everything that's social concern. This brings us close to something called communism. Although communism has a satisfying solution for the problems that I just named for the free market system, it's not wanted to have the government in control of everything. First the communists want a classless, stateless social organisation. This results in people not feeling a need to perform maximally, because there's no increase in reward to expect in return. This reward can be expressed in terms of money, social status and others. Due to people not performing at their peak, the optimum satisfaction and performance can't be expected of them, resulting in a society not as good as it could've been. Second there's the issue of the government controlling the market much. This induces that individual's ideas can't always be expressed much which prevents creativity from humans. This has a stalling effect on the development of technology, since many extraordinary technological ideas have been thought of by individuals. Another effect of the government controlling the market much is a smaller variety of products being available and the smaller the variety of products, the less likely people can be satisfied in their specific needs. And finally the lack of competition of companies supplying the same good disappears, often resulting in inefficiency. So the other extreme, communism, won't do either.

Finally I will combine the opinions I've expressed in the last two paragraphs to show requirements for a system in which technology has chances to evolve, but in which the humanity on short and long term is being guarded. In this system it's mandatory for humans to be fulfilled in the primary livings needs, regardless of their circumstances in life. This means that everyone should be supplied with a minimum income level, which can be calculated by summing up the minimum costs to live. Besides that everyone should have access to free education in any kind of form, free infrastructure access (telephone, roads, public transportation) and such. This will prevent a divide between poor and rich. Productivity and performance should be rewarded, and thus taxes should be equal at every income level, because a progressive tax system leads to people getting demotivated to work hard. Every good that's an essential primary need, needs to be controlled by the government. It'd still be possible to have goods of that kind on the free market, but with very strict laws to surely guarantee 100% quality (such as clean drinking water or an acceptable level of education). With concern to technology there should be an independent commission that judges the technology and that alerts the government when there are suspected dangers concerning technology coming up. This commission can also demonstrate new opportunities that'll benefit the whole country. There shouldn't be people in the government that are mainly in interest with technology and lacking negotiation skills, because these people haven't studied how to rule a country and might be biased to promote a technology of their preference most. Presence of democracy can make people going to vote to have the wanted people in the government. It's important to realise that technological disasters can never be 100% prevented and that it's not good to trust in the future technology to solve everything for us. Every decision should be based on the current availability of technology and excuses like “in the future we will have cleaner cars so we can pollute much now” should not be tolerated. 
